  #sol-item(1, "D", dense: true, pad: 25.31pt)[
    *Chapter 1.* B — data is the plural of datum and takes a plural verb in formal writing: were. Was, is and has are singular, which is common in speech but not the answer here.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "C", dense: true, pad: 25.31pt)[
    *Chapter 2.* C — a distance is one quantity and takes a singular verb: is. Are, were and have been would count the kilometres separately.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "B", dense: true, pad: 25.31pt)[
    *Chapter 3.* B — a number of means several and takes a plural verb: were. Was, is and has been would make it the number of, which is singular.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "A", dense: true, pad: 25.31pt)[
    *Chapter 4.* B — a past reporting verb moves the tense back, and the doing precedes the statement, so had done. Does and is doing keep a present tense, and will do points forward.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "D", dense: true, pad: 25.31pt)[
    *Chapter 5.* B — the ringing interrupted an action in progress, so the past continuous was addressing. Addresses is present, has addressed is perfect, and address is the base form.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "B", dense: true, pad: 25.31pt)[
    *Chapter 6.* B — the finishing came before a past arrival, so the past perfect had finished. The other three either keep a present tense or describe an action in progress.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "C", dense: true, pad: 25.31pt)[
    *Chapter 1.* B — after has the past participle is required, and the regular participle of work is worked. Work is the base form, working the present participle, and works the third-person present.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "A", dense: true, pad: 25.31pt)[
    *Chapter 2.* B — rigid means unbending, so its opposite is flexible. Stiff is a synonym, strong describes strength, and straight describes shape.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "D", dense: true, pad: 25.31pt)[
    *Chapter 3.* B — the blank follows no and needs a noun, and effect is the noun. Affect is the verb, affects is a present-tense verb, and effecting is a participle.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "B", dense: true, pad: 25.31pt)[
    *Chapter 4.* B — the proverb advises leaving a settled matter alone. Waking the dogs is the literal reading, and sleeping late is unrelated.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "C", dense: true, pad: 25.31pt)[
    *Chapter 5.* B — analysis is Greek and changes -is to -es: analyses. The other three are a regularised error, a form that does not exist, and a possessive.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "A", dense: true, pad: 25.31pt)[
    *Chapter 6.* B — although marks concession, which is the idea of doing something despite an obstacle. A gives reason, C gives time, and D gives purpose.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "D", dense: true, pad: 25.31pt)[
    *Chapter 1.* A — obstinate means refusing to change one's mind, which is stubborn. Obedient is its opposite, cheerful is unrelated, and timid describes fear rather than insistence.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "A", dense: true, pad: 25.31pt)[
    *Chapter 2.* B — concise means expressing much in few words. Lengthy is its opposite, and confusing and polite are unrelated.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "B", dense: true, pad: 25.31pt)[
    *Chapter 3.* B — the idiom means to study or work late. Lighting a lamp is the literal reading, and sleeping early is its opposite.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "C", dense: true, pad: 25.31pt)[
    *Chapter 4.* B — capable takes of plus a gerund. The other three use prepositions or forms that capable does not take.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "B", dense: true, pad: 25.31pt)[
    *Chapter 5.* B — the idiom means to disclose information. Wasting food and making a mess are literal readings, and cooking is unrelated.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "A", dense: true, pad: 25.31pt)[
    *Chapter 6.* B — a past reporting verb moves the tense back, and the repayment precedes the statement, so had repaid. Has repaid keeps a present tense, and will repay points forward.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "C", dense: true, pad: 25.31pt)[
    *Chapter 1.* B — hardly is followed by when in this inverted construction. Than pairs with no sooner, then is an adverb, and that does not follow hardly.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "D", dense: true, pad: 25.31pt)[
    *Chapter 2.* C — criteria is the plural of criterion and takes a plural verb: are. Is, was and has been treat it as singular, which is a common error.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "A", dense: true, pad: 25.31pt)[
    *Chapter 3.* B — beautifully modifies a verb or an adjective. Beautiful is an adjective, beauty a noun, and beautify a verb.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "B", dense: true, pad: 25.31pt)[
    *Chapter 4.* B — praise means to express approval, so its opposite is criticise. Commend and admire are synonyms, and greet is unrelated.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "C", dense: true, pad: 25.31pt)[
    *Chapter 5.* B — abundant means plentiful, so its opposite is scarce. Plentiful is a synonym, and cheap and hidden describe other qualities.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "D", dense: true, pad: 25.31pt)[
    *Chapter 6.* B — a colon introduces a list after a complete clause. A separates the verb from its object, C is a splice before a list, and D uses a semicolon where a list follows.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "C", dense: true, pad: 25.31pt)[
    *Chapter 1.* B — prudent means showing good judgement about the future. Reckless is its opposite, and wealth and silence are unrelated.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "D", dense: true, pad: 25.31pt)[
    *Chapter 2.* B — the stadium is the thing built, so the future passive is required. Will build and builds make it the builder, and is building is present continuous active.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "A", dense: true, pad: 25.31pt)[
